---
/**
 * Which governance properties the two checking tools can observe: the Tier 1
 * harness, against a running system, and the record conformance checker,
 * from written records. It was a page of its own at /evals/coverage, which
 * answered the same question as the measurement tiers ("how strong is this
 * evidence?") from the other end. It sits on that page now, after the
 * harness it extends.
 */
import {
  COVERAGE_LABELS,
  checkableProperties,
  coverageOf,
  type Coverage,
} from "../content/checkable-properties";

const ORDER: Coverage[] = ["both", "live-only", "emitted-only", "none"];

const counted = (coverage: Coverage) =>
  checkableProperties.filter((property) => coverageOf(property) === coverage)
    .length;

const probeCount = checkableProperties.reduce(
  (total, property) => total + property.probes.length,
  0,
);
---

<div class="stack-md coverage-matrix">
  <p>
    The harness tests a running system through an adapter that connects the two.
    The record conformance checker reads the decision records a system has
    already written. They were built separately and they overlap: {
      checkableProperties.length
    } governance properties, {probeCount} individual checks (probes) between them,
    and {counted("none")} properties with no check at all.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    Which side a property can be checked from matters. A live probe is available
    only to the operator of the system. A record probe is available to whoever
    holds the records, such as an auditor or a regulator. A property that can
    only be checked from inside is one everyone else has to take on trust.
  </p>
  <h3>Every property, checked from each side</h3>
  <p class="muted">
    One row per claim. A filled cell is a probe that exists. An empty cell is a
    side nobody can check from. Rows with neither are shaded. They are claims
    the framework argues for but this site cannot yet test.
  </p>
  <div
    class="mapping-table__wrapper"
    tabindex="0"
    role="region"
    aria-label="Live and record probes for each property"
  >
    <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--stack coverage-matrix__table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Property</th>
          <th scope="col"
            >Live probe<br /><span class="muted">against a running system</span
            ></th
          >
          <th scope="col"
            >Record probes<br /><span class="muted">from written records</span
            ></th
          >
          <th scope="col">Laws</th>
          <th scope="col">What the missing side would need</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ORDER.flatMap((coverage) =>
            checkableProperties
              .filter((property) => coverageOf(property) === coverage)
              .map((property) => {
                const live = property.probes.filter(
                  (p) => p.observes === "live",
                );
                const emitted = property.probes.filter(
                  (p) => p.observes === "emitted",
                );
                return (
                  <tr id={property.id} data-coverage={coverage}>
                    <th scope="row">
                      <strong>{property.title}</strong>
                      <span class="coverage-matrix__badge">
                        {COVERAGE_LABELS[coverage]}
                      </span>
                    </th>
                    <td data-label="Live probe">
                      {live.length > 0 ? (
                        live.map((probe) => (
                          <div class="coverage-matrix__probe">
                            <code>{probe.id}</code>
                            <span class="coverage-matrix__probe-what">
                              {probe.what}
                            </span>
                          </div>
                        ))
                      ) : (
                        <span class="coverage-matrix__empty">none</span>
                      )}
                    </td>
                    <td data-label="Record probes">
                      {emitted.length > 0 ? (
                        emitted.map((probe) => (
                          <div class="coverage-matrix__probe">
                            <code>{probe.id}</code>
                            <span class="coverage-matrix__probe-what">
                              {probe.what}
                            </span>
                          </div>
                        ))
                      ) : (
                        <span class="coverage-matrix__empty">none</span>
                      )}
                    </td>
                    <td data-label="Laws">
                      <p class="pill-list pill-list--wrap">
                        {property.laws.map((law) => (
                          <a
                            class="pill pill--ghost"
                            href={`/standards/laws#${law}`}
                          >
                            {law.replace("law-", "").toUpperCase()}
                          </a>
                        ))}
                      </p>
                    </td>
                    <td
                      data-label="What the missing side would need"
                      class="coverage-matrix__gap"
                    >
                      {property.gap ?? "—"}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                );
              }),
          )
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h3>Every check maps to a claim</h3>
  <p>
    A check that nobody can map back to a claim is only a number. A claim nobody
    can map to a check is only an assertion. A test enforces the list: every
    harness check and every conformance finding must belong to exactly one
    property here, or the build fails. A new check cannot be added without
    saying what it shows. A property cannot be dropped quietly because nothing
    checks it.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    The list is in the <a
      href="https://github.com/zz-plant/ethotechnics.org/blob/main/src/content/checkable-properties.ts"
      >public source code</a
    >.
  </p>
</div>
